%[text] Option A - Compare Denoising Filters
%[text] Question: How well do a 3x3 median filter, a Gaussian filter (sigma = 1.5), and a combined 3x3 median + Gaussian filter (sigma = 1) clean up rice.png when it has both Gaussian and salt & pepper noise?
%[text] Data: MATLAB sample image rice.png (256x256 grayscale uint8).
clear; clc; close all;

% Load reference image
Iref = imread('rice.png');

% Add Gaussian and salt & pepper noise
rng(42);
Id = imnoise(imnoise(Iref, 'gaussian', 0, 0.01), 'salt & pepper', 0.02);

% Apply the three filters
I_med = medfilt2(Id, [3 3]);
I_gauss = imgaussfilt(Id, 1.5);
I_both = imgaussfilt(medfilt2(Id, [3 3]), 1);

% Save processed images to results folder
imwrite(I_med, 'results/median_3x3.png');
imwrite(I_gauss, 'results/gaussian_1_5.png');
imwrite(I_both, 'results/median_gaussian.png');

% Compute PSNR and SSIM against Iref
psnr_noisy = psnr(Id, Iref);
psnr_med   = psnr(I_med, Iref);
psnr_gauss = psnr(I_gauss, Iref);
psnr_both  = psnr(I_both, Iref);

ssim_noisy = ssim(Id, Iref);
ssim_med   = ssim(I_med, Iref);
ssim_gauss = ssim(I_gauss, Iref);
ssim_both  = ssim(I_both, Iref);

% Display results table
Filter = ["Noisy (Id)"; "3x3 Median"; "Gaussian (sigma=1.5)"; "3x3 Median + Gaussian (sigma=1)"];
PSNR = [psnr_noisy; psnr_med; psnr_gauss; psnr_both];
SSIM = [ssim_noisy; ssim_med; ssim_gauss; ssim_both];
T = table(Filter, PSNR, SSIM);
disp(T); %[output:0dd93b4b]
writetable(T, 'results/results_table.csv');

% Show full images (Ref, Noisy, Median, Gaussian, Combined)
f1 = figure; %[output:8988cd89]
montage({Iref, Id, I_med, I_gauss, I_both}, 'Size', [1 5]); %[output:8988cd89]
title('Full Images: Reference | Noisy | 3x3 Median | Gaussian (1.5) | Median + Gaussian (1)'); %[output:8988cd89]
exportgraphics(f1, 'results/full_comparison.png'); %[output:8988cd89]

% Show zoomed 70x70 region (rows 80:149, cols 80:149)
Z_ref   = Iref(80:149, 80:149);
Z_noisy = Id(80:149, 80:149);
Z_med   = I_med(80:149, 80:149);
Z_gauss = I_gauss(80:149, 80:149);
Z_both  = I_both(80:149, 80:149);

f2 = figure; %[output:6f56079f]
montage({Z_ref, Z_noisy, Z_med, Z_gauss, Z_both}, 'Size', [1 5]); %[output:6f56079f]
title('Zoomed 70x70: Reference | Noisy | 3x3 Median | Gaussian (1.5) | Median + Gaussian (1)'); %[output:6f56079f]
exportgraphics(f2, 'results/zoomed_comparison.png'); %[output:6f56079f]

%[text] Discussion and Limitations:
%[text] Which filter I prefer:
%[text] I prefer the combined 3x3 median filter followed by the Gaussian filter (sigma = 1). It gives the best PSNR and SSIM values and removes both kinds of noise without ruining the edges of the rice grains.
%[text] Remaining noise and blur:
%[text] \- 3x3 Median filter: Gets rid of almost all the black and white salt-and-pepper dots and keeps the grain edges sharp, because extreme outlier pixels never become the middle value when sorted. However, grainy Gaussian noise is still visible across the background and inside the rice grains.
%[text] \- Gaussian filter (sigma = 1.5): Smooths out the Gaussian graininess, but because it is a linear average, it smears the salt-and-pepper spikes into blurry grey blotches and makes the rice grain edges fuzzy.
%[text] \- 3x3 Median + Gaussian (sigma = 1): Doing the median filter first strips out the salt-and-pepper spikes before they can get blurred, and then the mild Gaussian filter smooths out the remaining Gaussian noise while keeping the rice grains clear.
%[text] Limitation of the comparison:
%[text] PSNR and SSIM require a clean reference image (Iref) to compare against, which we do not have in real-world camera applications. Also, PSNR and SSIM only measure pixel and structural similarity rather than checking if a later step, like counting or segmenting the rice grains, actually works better.

